>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Micropiles</span>
        <h1>Micropiles —<br /><em>The Drilled Option</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">"Micro" refers to the diameter, not the capacity. What they are, how a grout-to-soil bond carries real structural load, and when hard ground rules out a driven or torqued pier.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Micropiles sound specialized because they are — but hearing the word on an estimate doesn't automatically mean your soil is unusually bad. It means something about the ground, the access, or both, ruled out the simpler options.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>The Mechanism: <em>Grout-to-Soil Bond, Not Bearing</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A micropile is a small-diameter drilled and grouted foundation element, typically 3 to 12 inches across, that transfers structural load to competent ground through a specific mechanism: the bond between grout and the surrounding soil.</p>
          <p>Three parts work together — steel reinforcement (usually a threaded bar or steel tube) carrying the structural load, cement grout filling the drilled hole and bonding to the surrounding soil, and the drilled borehole itself creating the space where that load transfer happens. The steel provides strength, the grout transfers load from steel to soil, and the hole is what makes the whole thing possible in ground nothing else can get through.</p>
          <p>Because that load transfer happens through a drilled-and-grouted bond rather than driving or torque, there are very few soil conditions a micropile technically can't work in — harder soil generally means a shorter, more efficient pile, while looser soil means going deeper to develop the same bond capacity. That versatility is exactly why it's tempting to reach for a micropile on almost anything. It shouldn't be: micropiles are specialized equipment and specialized labor, and depending on the soil, they can be one of the more expensive options in the toolkit. Being able to use a micropile on a job isn't the same as it being the right or most economical choice for it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>Why Bond Strength Depends on <em>the Grouting Method</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">The mechanism is fundamentally different from a <a href="/helical-piers-deep-dive" class="body-link">helical</a> or <a href="/push-piers-deep-dive" class="body-link">push pier</a>, and it's the reason micropiles exist as a separate tool rather than just a smaller pier.</p>
          <p>Capacity comes almost entirely from the bond between grout and soil along the shaft's full length — technically called skin friction or shaft resistance — not from bearing at the tip. The pile tip does rest on soil at the bottom of the hole, but design typically doesn't count on that end bearing at all; it's a conservative approach that relies on what can actually be predicted and verified.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Grouting method sets the bond</span>
            <p>The Federal Highway Administration classifies micropile grouting into four types, and the method changes the bond strength you get from the same soil. Type A is gravity-placed grout — no injection pressure — and produces the lowest bond values. Type B is pressure-grouted during casing withdrawal (roughly 70–145 psi), forcing grout into pores and irregularities in the borehole wall for meaningfully higher bond than Type A. Types C and D add one or multiple post-grouting pressure phases (Type D up to 290–1,160 psi) for the highest bond values, used where capacity requirements or difficult soil call for it. Per FHWA reference guidance (FHWA-NHI-05-039, Table 5-3), ultimate bond strength across these methods and soil types ranges roughly from 5 psi in soft clay to 56+ psi in dense sand and gravel — more than a tenfold spread from the same physical hole in different ground.</p>
          </div>
          <p>Shaft friction also works in both directions. A micropile resists uplift (tension) about as well as it resists downward load (compression), which makes it useful for wind or seismic uplift in a way a standard push pier — compression-only unless specifically engineered otherwise — isn't. The tradeoff is lateral capacity: the small diameter gives a micropile little resistance to bending, so where lateral load matters, piles are often installed at an angle (battered) rather than vertically, resisting sideways force through axial tension and compression in the incline instead.</p>
          <details class="deeper">
            <summary class="deeper-toggle">
              <span>Going Deeper — For the Technically Curious</span>
              <span class="deeper-toggle-icon">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="deeper-body">
              <p>Grout-to-soil bond is only half the load path — the other half is the bond between the grout and the steel reinforcement itself, which is what actually transfers load from the bar to the grout in the first place. That bond isn't a single fixed number the way it can look in a table. FHWA's micropile manual cites typical ultimate ranges (roughly <strong>145–254 psi</strong> for a smooth bar or pipe, <strong>290–508 psi</strong> for a deformed, threaded bar, per ACI 318), but ICC-ES evaluation reports for specific bar products and Post-Tensioning Institute guidance set their own values too — none of which overrides the judgment of the engineer actually stamping the design.</p>
              <p>In practice, most micropile contractors — TLS included — rely on delegated design: a specialty engineer, not a table lookup, sets the actual bond value for the job. Bond strength depends heavily on the drilling means and methods actually used on that hole, not just the soil type on paper, and matching the design value to how the pile is really going to be drilled and grouted is a large part of what that engineer is doing. It's part art, part science — which is exactly why good engineering on the design side makes a real difference to how the finished pile performs.</p>
              <p>On the inspection side: the IBC doesn't have a subsection written specifically for micropiles. They fall under <strong>Section 1705.8</strong>, Cast-in-Place Deep Foundations — the same category used for any drilled-and-grouted element — which requires continuous inspection of the drilling operation and verification of placement, diameter, length, embedment, and grout volume.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: FHWA-NHI-05-039, p. 5-9 (citing ACI 318) — one published reference point among several a design engineer may draw on, not a universal value; IBC Section 1705.8, Cast-in-Place Deep Foundations.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>Slower, Messier, and <em>Worth It When Ground Demands It</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Installation drills a borehole to design depth (the method — rotary, percussion, or auger — depends on what's actually being drilled through), places the steel reinforcement, then grouts it in place, either by gravity or under pressure depending on the bond strength the job requires.</p>
          <p>One common variation in New Mexico is the self-drilling approach, sometimes called Geo-Drill: instead of drilling the hole, pulling the drill string, and inserting separate reinforcement afterward, the drill string itself — a hollow bar with a sacrificial bit at the tip — stays in the ground and becomes the reinforcing steel. Grout is pumped through the hollow bar as it drills, so the hole is drilled and the reinforcement placed in a single pass rather than two.</p>
          <p>It's messier and slower than most of the other tools in the toolkit — drilling produces spoils and wet conditions, and a job needing several micropiles can run multiple days rather than one. Because capacity depends on bonding to whatever the shaft passes through, the relevant question when a pier can't reach bearing ground isn't just depth — it's whether the ground along that depth can actually be drilled through and bonded to.</p>
          <p>How hard that question is to answer depends entirely on what's actually down there. A uniform soil profile is a straightforward drill. Loose soil sitting over cobbles sitting over more loose soil — genuinely difficult, interbedded ground — is one of the harder conditions to work through, and it forces real means-and-methods decisions on the spot: hollow bar or a cased hole, which grouting approach fits the layer being drilled through right now. That's exactly why contractor experience is paramount on a micropile job, and why the design engineer's experience matters just as much — competent decisions on both sides of that line are what actually control cost and outcome, not a one-size-fits-all approach.</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a><a href="/glossary#micropile" class="body-link">Glossary: Micropile →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

